<!--
  turns off the diagnostic tables ClickHouse writes about itself (#2795, #2815).

  the image's default server config enables most of them, and each one is a
  MergeTree table that takes an insert every few seconds. on a small host with a
  slow disk (a Raspberry Pi 5 on an SD card was the measured case) the merges
  those inserts cause never catch up: two idle servers sat at ~190% and ~320% CPU
  with every MergeMutate thread busy on text_log and asynchronous_metric_log.
  rolter reads none of them.

  one file serves three servers: rolter's own clickhouse (docker-compose.yml, and
  through it the team shape and the dogfood stack), signoz-clickhouse
  (docker-compose.signoz.yml, 25.12.5) and the e2e stack's (integration/e2e).
  signoz reads no `system.*_log` table either: its query service, schema migrator
  and collector read system.tables, columns, disks, clusters, databases,
  mutations and distributed_ddl_queue, and the only log table either binary names
  is `query_log`, for an optional receiver this overlay's collector does not run.
  `aggregated_zookeeper_log`, `background_schedule_pool_log` and
  `zookeeper_connection_log` only exist on a server that has a zookeeper and
  replicated tables, which is signoz-clickhouse's case: 25.12.5 kept writing them
  with every other table in this list off (#2909).

  `query_log` is kept on purpose: the control plane's ux_events ingest and the
  troubleshooting docs read it, and on signoz-clickhouse it is how an operator
  sees which signoz query is slow. it is the one diagnostic table worth its disk.
  `crash_log` is kept too: it only gets a row when the server crashes.

  `remove="1"` deletes the element from the merged server config, which is how
  ClickHouse documents turning one of these off. it is a no-op where the image's
  version has no such table, so the list can name tables a newer image adds.

  mounted as a single file in /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/, never as the
  directory: the image ships docker_related_config.xml there (the listen
  addresses), and a directory mount would hide it.

  this only stops new tables being created. a volume that already ran with the
  default config keeps the ones it has; docs/dev-docs/deployment/docker.md shows
  how to drop them.
-->
<clickhouse>
  <aggregated_zookeeper_log remove="1" />
  <asynchronous_insert_log remove="1" />
  <asynchronous_metric_log remove="1" />
  <backup_log remove="1" />
  <background_schedule_pool_log remove="1" />
  <blob_storage_log remove="1" />
  <error_log remove="1" />
  <latency_log remove="1" />
  <metric_log remove="1" />
  <opentelemetry_span_log remove="1" />
  <part_log remove="1" />
  <processors_profile_log remove="1" />
  <query_metric_log remove="1" />
  <query_thread_log remove="1" />
  <query_views_log remove="1" />
  <s3queue_log remove="1" />
  <session_log remove="1" />
  <text_log remove="1" />
  <trace_log remove="1" />
  <zookeeper_connection_log remove="1" />
</clickhouse>
